## What blocked the work

`scripts/kiro-hooks/guard-secrets.sh write` flags ordinary Rust text as a probable API key. Its regex is `re_[A-Za-z0-9_-]{20,}`, matched case-insensitively. This hook also runs on `fs_write`, because its matcher `write` matches that name. I reproduced the block by running the script directly on a short Rust test sample. Writes were refused across `fs_write`, `fs_append` and `str_replace`, so I couldn't add the T02 tests.

I did not reword the code to avoid the scanner, and I did not write through the shell, since both would bypass a guard. The one earlier `str_replace` that succeeded removed the `FailureReason` block from `status.rs`. The second edit was refused, so I restored that file from git. Git showed no uncommitted changes there before this session, so nothing else was lost.

**Unblock:** Brighton should anchor the key patterns so they need a real token boundary, for example `(^|[^A-Za-z0-9_])re_[A-Za-z0-9_-]{20,}`, and do the same for `sk-` and `gsk_`. The hook should also print its reason. The full diagnosis is in `docs/kiro-log/run-lifecycle-blockers.md`.

## Blockers

- **Write guard (T02 onward):** see above.
- **T02:** `FailureReason` tests not written. The enum is in `crates/common/src/status.rs`, after the test module, which may trip Clippy's `items_after_test_module` lint. Not checked this session.
- **T03:** the proptests and `status_after_event` are not written.
